WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:03.167
Why can a hotter pan make
a drop of water last longer?

2
00:00:03.500 --> 00:00:07.733
On a hot metal surface,
water can touch the metal
and boil away quickly.

3
00:00:08.000 --> 00:00:10.233
Heat it far enough,
and something changes.

4
00:00:10.500 --> 00:00:13.633
The bottom of the drop
turns to vapor almost instantly.

5
00:00:13.900 --> 00:00:17.733
That vapor forms a cushion,
lifting the rest of the water
off the metal.

6
00:00:18.100 --> 00:00:21.033
Now heat must cross
a poorly conducting gas layer.

7
00:00:21.267 --> 00:00:24.367
The hotter surface has created
a barrier to its own heat.

8
00:00:24.767 --> 00:00:26.733
This is the Leidenfrost effect.

9
00:00:27.000 --> 00:00:29.833
It doesn’t mean hotter always
makes water last longer.

10
00:00:30.100 --> 00:00:33.100
The transition depends on
the surface and the drop.

11
00:00:33.367 --> 00:00:36.233
Above it, more heat can
speed evaporation again.

12
00:00:36.467 --> 00:00:41.400
The surprise is that
a hotter surface can give water
less direct contact with the heat.
